<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="wikitree">WikiTree, opened for the first time</h2>
  <p class="dek">Asked on 13 September 2026 whether it had ever been looked at, this archive had to answer
    no &mdash; the word appears nowhere in the project. Thirteen Booyzen profiles were then read. It is a
    <b>user-contributed tree and therefore a lead and not a source</b>, exactly like the eleven above it
    in that table. But it differs from them in one way that matters: <b>profiles carry a Sources section,
    and several of these cite documents by reference.</b></p>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The accounting below is deliberately two-directional, because a tree is only useful if you are
      willing to say where it beats you and where you beat it.</p>

    <h3>Where it is ahead of this archive</h3>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:15rem">What WikiTree holds</th><th>What this archive held</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>Heese, <em>Suid-Afrikaanse Geslagsregisters</em>, Deel 1, <span class="m">p. 331</span></b></td>
              <td><a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Question 0</a> named the published Geslagsregisters as the
                route left for generation seven and this page marked it <b>"Not yet followed"</b>. There was
                no page number. <b>Now there is one.</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>David Schalk Booijsen married <b>Adriana Christina Viljoen, 3 November 1828, Cradock</b></td>
              <td>The wife, but not the marriage &mdash; no date and no place.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Henry James Booyzen <b>born 12 August 1887</b>; married Dorothea Regina Barry
                <b>17 June 1912 at Elliot</b></td>
              <td>"c. 1887", worked back from age 69 on a burial register, and "in 1912" with no day and no
                church.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Jacobus Nicolaas Booyzen married <b>Jacomina Jacoba Jansen van Rensburg, 12 February 1866,
                Middelburg</b></td>
              <td>Nothing. He was the eldest name on the 1863 notice and an estate number, with no wife at
                all. <em>Caution: the profile's own source line dates that marriage 1899, not 1866.</em></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Where this archive is ahead of it</h3>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:15rem">What WikiTree holds</th><th>What this archive holds</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Hendrik Stefanus Booysen as a son of <b>Anna Maria van der Merwe</b> &mdash; while the
                profile's own prose hedges <em>"Elizabetha ? (Anna Maria ?) van der Merwe"</em></td>
              <td><b>Resolved.</b> His 1908 death notice, TAB estate 14380, names his mother
                <b>ELIZABETHA BOOYSEN, BORN VAN DER MERWE</b>. Another researcher reached the same
                ambiguity and left it standing in a question mark; the notice settles it.
                <a href={u("/documents/#hendrik-1908")}>The notice &rarr;</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Charlotta Elizabetha Dormehl, <b>1841&ndash;1902</b></td>
              <td>Born about October 1841 at <b>Graaff-Reinet</b>; died <b>6 February 1902 at Driefontein,
                Groot Marico</b>, with two recorded married surnames.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><em>Hendrik Stiphanis</em> Booyzen, 1830&ndash;1905, of Graaff-Reinet</td>
              <td><b>Not this family.</b> Son of Jan Hendrik Booysen and Cornelia Margaretha Voetzé &mdash;
                and the profile has him <b>married at the age of twelve</b>, which is the kind of thing a
                shared tree carries in public for years.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>And one head-on contradiction</h3>
    <blockquote><b>WikiTree gives Petrus Jacobus Booysen a different father.</b></blockquote>
    <p>Its profile <span class="m">Booysen-267</span> is unmistakably our man &mdash; born before
      <b>21 September 1788</b>, died <b>1 September 1876</b>, the same two dates this archive carries. It
      makes his parents <b>JAN (Boijens) BOOYSEN and MAGDALENA DE BEER</b>. This archive makes them
      <b>GERTHARDUS LODEWIKUS BOOIJSEN and MAGDALENA DE BEER</b>, and not on the strength of a tree: the
      names are in <b>line 3 of his 1876 Rustenburg Sterfkennis</b>, No. 678, which this archive has read
      and imaged. Same mother, same son, two different husbands.</p>
    <p><b>The reason this is not simply a tree being wrong</b> is that this archive has already met that
      Jan. Question 0 records a near-miss it deliberately declined to adopt &mdash; <span class="m">KAB NCD
      2/1: 49</span>, a notarial will of 1793 for <b>"Jan Booysen, married to the late Magdalena de
      Beer"</b> &mdash; and set it aside as <em>"wrong husband's name"</em>. Another researcher, working
      independently, has put that same Jan in that same position.</p>
    <h3>Then the book itself was read, and it went the other way</h3>
    <div class="note">
      <b>Withdrawn, 14 September 2026.</b> What stood here for a day was a section headed
      <em>"Following it up the tree settles it &mdash; and the archive was right"</em>. It argued that
      Jan Booysen's wife and this archive's Magdelena Elisabeth de Beer were <em>two different women</em>,
      fifty years apart, and that the tree had merged them. <b>Reading the source settled it the other
      way.</b> The reasoning is kept on <a href={u("/corrections/")}>Corrections</a> rather than quietly
      deleted.
    </div>
    <p>A member of G2G, <b>Pieter van Zyl</b>, answered within a day and named the book as freely
      downloadable: <b>de Villiers, <em>Geslacht-register der oude Kaapsche familien</em> (1893)</b>
      &mdash; the original of which <em>Genealogies of Old Cape Families</em> is the revised edition, so
      the two references this archive was chasing turned out to be one book. It was downloaded and read
      rather than taken on trust.</p>
    <p><b>Page 34, de Beer.</b> Zacharias de Beer, baptised 5 March 1719, married Anna Pretorius on
      12 May 1743. He is the <b>fifth</b> child of the progenitor Matthijs Andries de Beer &mdash; so the
      code is <b>b5</b>, not the b6 WikiTree carries. His daughters include <b>c2 Magdalena, baptised
      16 October 1745</b>, with no onward reference, and <b>c6 Magdalena, baptised 16 October 1754,
      "zie Booijsen"</b>. <b>The cross-reference to this family is on the second one</b>, and 1754 is the
      birth year this archive has always carried for its own Magdelena Elisabeth &mdash; a figure its own
      page admitted was "off the family tree with no source stated". It is now sourced. They are one
      woman, not two.</p>
    <blockquote><b>And then page 78 names her husband, and it is not ours.</b></blockquote>
    <p>The Booijsen section gives <b>Jan, baptised 29 January 1741</b>, burgher at Drakenstein, married
      <b>21 January 1770 to Magdalena de Beer</b> and remarried <b>15 December 1793</b>. His seventh
      child is <b>"Petrus Jacobus, gedoopt 21 September 1788, gehuwd met Anna Maria van der Merwe"</b>,
      and that man's first child is <b>"David Schalk, gedoopt 4 Januari 1807"</b>.</p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:14rem">Checked against this archive</th><th>de Villiers 1893</th><th class="m">Agrees?</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Petrus Jacobus baptised c. 21 Sep 1788</td><td>21 September 1788</td><td class="m">yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>His wife Anna Maria van der Merwe</td><td>Anna Maria van der Merwe</td><td class="m">yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>His son David Schalk baptised 4 Jan 1807</td><td>4 Januari 1807</td><td class="m">yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>His mother Magdalena de Beer</td><td>Magdalena de Beer</td><td class="m">yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>His father Gerthardus Lodewikus Booijsen</b></td><td><b>Jan Booijsen</b></td><td class="m"><b>NO</b></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p><b>Four fields out of five agree, which is what makes the fifth serious.</b> This is certainly the
      same man: there is <b>exactly one</b> "Petrus Jacobus, gedoopt 21 September 1788" in the whole book,
      and <b>exactly one</b> David Schalk of 4 January 1807. And the word <em>Lodewikus</em> does not
      occur anywhere in the volume. The only Gerhardus-Lodewijk name in the Booijsen pages is a cousin
      baptised in 1770, eighteen years old when Petrus Jacobus was born.</p>
    <blockquote><b>So the archive's generation seven now rests on one line of one document, against a
      published genealogy built from the baptism registers.</b> The 1876 Sterfkennis names
      "Gerthardus Lödewikus Booijsen"; it was written about a man of eighty-eight, and the field it is
      in asks a grieving family for the names of parents who had died some seventy years earlier. That
      is exactly the field that goes wrong on a death notice. <b>It has not been overturned and nothing
      has been rewritten &mdash; but it is no longer the only evidence in the room.</b>
      <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Question 0 &rarr;</a> ·
      <a href={u("/corrections/")}>What was withdrawn &rarr;</a></blockquote>
    <p><b>Where this archive is still ahead.</b> de Villiers gives Petrus Jacobus only two children,
      David Schalk and Magdalena Johanna. The 1876 Sterfkennis names <b>eight</b>. The printed
      genealogy is better on the generation above and worse on the generation below, which is the
      ordinary shape of a book compiled from registers in 1893.</p>

    <p><b>Nothing has been changed on the strength of it.</b> A document this archive has read outranks a
      tree, and the 1876 notice stands. But the disagreement is now recorded, it has a published book and
      an exact page to be settled against, and the 1793 will has stopped being a curiosity.
      <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Question 0 &rarr;</a> ·
      <a href={u("/searched/")}>What was searched &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="library">A library is not an archive, and this archive did not know that mattered</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>For three weeks this project has treated &ldquo;the Cape Town holdings&rdquo; as one place. It is
      at least two, and the second one has been invisible to every index in use here.</p>
    <p><b>The Western Cape Archives, 72 Roeland Street</b>, holds public records &mdash; estates, wills,
      inventories, the Master's series. That is where nearly every errand on this site points, and it is
      what NAAIRS indexes.</p>
    <p><b>The National Library of South Africa, 5 Queen Victoria Street</b>, holds manuscripts: private
      papers somebody gave to a library. Separate building, separate reader's ticket, separate request.
      <b>MSB751, the GA Kolbe Collection, is there</b> &mdash; four photocopied pages including a letter
      from London of 23 August 1830 &mdash; and this archive found it <em>by accident</em>, while
      searching a national index for something else entirely.</p>
    <blockquote>A family's papers do not sit where its records sit. <b>The records are where the state
      kept them; the papers are wherever a descendant happened to give them.</b></blockquote>
    <p class="cap">Which raises a question this site cannot answer and should ask out loud: <b>how many
      other manuscript collections mention these families in libraries nobody here has thought to
      search?</b> MSB751 was donated in 1985 by a Dr Jean Griffiths of Somerset West, who is not a name
      in this archive and may have had more.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="shut">Six kinds of shut door, and the differences matter</h2>
  <p class="dek">Whether a host answers is a mechanical question, and the table below it answers that.
    This is the other one: <b>why</b> a source is out of reach. Six of them are, for six different
    reasons, and treating them as one thing &mdash; &ldquo;not available online&rdquo; &mdash; would
    throw away everything useful about them.</p>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:15rem">Source</th><th style="width:11rem">Why it is shut</th><th>What follows</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><b>TANAP</b><br /><span class="cap">Cape estate inventories, transcribed entire</span></td>
          <td><b>Gone.</b> The host stopped resolving mid-session.</td>
          <td class="cap"><b>The worst kind, because it was solved and is now unsolved.</b> Somebody did
            the work of transcribing these and the work has fallen off the internet. The Internet
            Archive was itself down the day this was found, so there was not even a copy to fall back
            on. The documents survive at Roeland Street; the transcriptions may not survive anywhere.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><b>HathiTrust</b><br /><span class="cap">London trade directories</span></td>
          <td><b>Guarded.</b> A bot check stands in front of it.</td>
          <td class="cap"><b>This archive does not go around a bot check</b>, and records that as the
            reason rather than as a failure. The material is there and a human may read it. That is a
            defensible position for a library to take and this is not a complaint.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><b>Google Books</b><br /><span class="cap">the same directories</span></td>
          <td><b>Priced out at zero.</b> The API returns a daily quota of nought to anonymous callers.</td>
          <td class="cap">Not a wall so much as a toll gate with no price on it. Recorded because the
            failure looks like an outage and is not one &mdash; and because an hour was spent before
            the difference was clear.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><b>FamilySearch</b><br /><span class="cap">Dutch Reformed baptism registers</span></td>
          <td><b>Bounded.</b> Every register in it begins in <b>1843</b>.</td>
          <td class="cap"><b>The most expensive of the six, because it does not announce itself.</b> A
            search returns nothing and looks like an absence; it is an edge. Weeks of work on this
            family produced nothing older than the 1840s and the reason was never the registers &mdash;
            they exist. It was where the filming started.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><b>Transvaal estate files</b><br /><span class="cap">TAB MHG</span></td>
          <td><b>Bound.</b> The paper is sewn into volumes.</td>
          <td class="cap">A physical fact, given plainly by an archivist who could have said nothing.
            Bound paper will not lie on a scanner. <b>Nothing about this is a policy and nobody is
            withholding anything</b>, which is worth saying because for weeks this archive assumed the
            obstacle was willingness or staff time.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><b>NAAIRS</b><br /><span class="cap">the national index</span></td>
          <td><b>Intermittent.</b> &ldquo;The connection to the Search Manager Server is inactive.&rdquo;</td>
          <td class="cap">The only one of the six that will probably fix itself. Work parked against it
            is recorded as <b>an outage and not as a negative</b> &mdash; because a search that could
            not run has found nothing, and a search that ran and found nothing has found something.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><b>Put together they are an argument, and it is not the
    obvious one.</b> Almost nothing on this list is anybody refusing anything. Two are technical
    boundaries nobody thought to publish, one is a physical property of old paper, one is a library
    defending itself from robots, one is a price, and one is a server having an afternoon.
    <b>The single biggest obstacle to this archive is not access. It is that the edges of collections
    are undocumented</b> &mdash; and an undocumented edge is indistinguishable, from a distance, from
    an empty archive. This site has made that mistake four times in a week and caught it four times,
    which is not a ratio anybody should rely on.</p>

  <h2 id="state">Are these sources still there?</h2>
  <p class="dek">Checked {srcState.checked}. Every external host this archive points at &mdash;
    linked, or merely named in a sentence &mdash; asked whether it still answers.</p>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:16px">
    <p><b>This page exists because one of them went out mid-session.</b>
      <span class="m">databases.tanap.net</span> holds the full transcriptions of the Cape estate
      inventories, including the 1781 inventory that names eleven enslaved people of this family's
      household. It stopped resolving between one search and the next, and this site went on citing it
      as though it were live. <b>An archive that cites what it cannot reach, without saying so, is
      making a promise it cannot keep.</b></p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:18rem">Host</th><th style="width:8rem">State</th><th style="width:5rem">Cited</th><th>What that means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {srcState.rows.filter(r => r.state !== "up").map(r => (
          <tr>
            <td class="m">{r.host}</td>
            <td class="m" style={`color:${r.state === "dark" ? "#B04A16" : r.state === "mail only" ? "#12703F" : "#E9A020"}`}>{r.state}</td>
            <td class="m">{r.cited}&times;</td>
            <td class="cap">{r.why}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px">{srcState.rows.filter(r => r.state === "up").length} further
    hosts answer normally and are not listed.</p>

  <p class="note" style="margin-top:18px"><b>The check was wrong before it was right, and the way it was
    wrong is worth keeping.</b> Its first run called three live government domains dead, because it asked
    for an address record and <b>a department can be perfectly alive with nothing but mail</b>.
    <span class="m">dsac.gov.za</span> &mdash; where every letter to Pretoria has gone, cited
    twenty-five times on this site &mdash; has no website at all. On the strength of that first answer
    this archive was one edit from publishing that the Pietermaritzburg addresses were dead.
    <b>They accept mail.</b> The check now asks for the MX before it says dark.</p>

  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px"><b>It is deliberately not part of the build.</b> A network probe
    inside a build fails when a government website is having an afternoon, which has nothing to do with
    the change being built &mdash; and a check that fails for the wrong reason is a check people learn to
    ignore. It runs on demand and writes what it finds here.
    <a href={u("/corrections/")}>One published address was wrong &rarr;</a></p>
